     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Confirming it doesn't change the problem. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the labeled maximum, and I can't think of a clinical situation where that dose is appropriate. It would expose the patient to serious risk of GI hemorrhage, kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm. It also adds no analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit, because ibuprofen's effect plateaus well below that dose.\n\nYou have some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the limit.** I can enter 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day, the maximum) or 400 to 800 mg PO three to four times daily right away.\n- **Get a second opinion.** If you have a specific reason for this dose, such as a protocol I don't know about, talk to your attending or the clinical pharmacist. They can review it and enter it through the proper channels.\n- **Reconsider the analgesic plan.** If 3,200 mg/day isn't controlling the pain or inflammation, adding acetaminophen or choosing a different agent is safer than escalating ibuprofen.\n\nIf you want one of the within-limit doses, tell me which and I'll enter it."
      }
